{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1785260962,"nonce":"BsLAveJ7px82QLGxRUeptqs6u68=","title":"ADR-0062: settle_change_set's future is not provably Send, so the fold runs on a blocking thread","message":"Found while wiring s4. `settle::settle_change_set`'s future fails rustc 1.97's higher-ranked `Send` check — `apply_written` does `buffer_unordered` over `\u0026Job\u003c'_\u003e`, and the check collapses with \"not general enough\" for `\u0026BlobHash`, `\u0026TreeHash`, `slice::Iter\u003c'_, Job\u003c'_\u003e\u003e`. No call-site dodge reaches it: `Box::pin`, coercing to `dyn Future + Send`, owning every parameter, and a `fn assert_send\u003cT: Send\u003e` probe all fail.\n\nAxum requires `Send`, so `workspaced.rs` currently drives the fold on `spawn_blocking` + `Handle::block_on`. It works, and it **occupies a blocking-pool thread for the duration of the fold** and would stall on a `current_thread` runtime with nothing else driving it.\n\n**The fix is in `settle.rs`**: make `Job.comps` a `Vec\u003cString\u003e`, or buffer over owned jobs. `S3Storage::ingest_tree` already does it the working way, which is the evidence that the shape is fine.\n\n- [ ] `settle_change_set`'s future is `Send`, proven by a `fn assert_send\u003cT: Send\u003e(_: T) {}` call on it in a test\n- [ ] The `spawn_blocking` + `block_on` workaround in `workspaced.rs` is removed\n- [ ] No regression in the fold's concurrency (the `buffer_unordered` fan-out stays)","files":null}]}